                <head>PE–38. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>Airgram 97 to Lima, November 10, 1959, authorized the Embassy in Peru to
                    negotiate with the Peruvian Government for the sale of $12 million (including
                    one half the cost of transportation) worth of United States surplus agricultural
                    commodities under Title I of Public Law 480, the Agricultural Trade development
                    and Assistance Act of 1954. Enclosed was a draft sales agreement.
                    (411.2341/11-1059) The proposed agreement was signed at Lima on February 12,
                    1960. Of the total value of approximately $12 million, 45 percent was to be
                    loaned to Peru for economic development; 25 percent to finance loans to private
                    enterprises in Peru under Section 104(e), the Cooley Amendment to the Act; and
                    30 percent was to be retained for United States Government <pb facs="1198"
                        n="1110" type="typeset" xml:id="pg1110"/> use. For the text of the
                    agreement, see 11 UST 186. For Section 104(e),
                    which provided that up to 25 percent of local currency from Title I sales he
                    made available for loans to U.S. and to private investors through the
                    Export-Import Bank, see 71 State 345.</p>
            </div>
